title: 'C-c z : skg-zippy-mode, typing without C-c'
pid: efd2d81e-af20-4ae1-b9eb-0f167bc4a9e4
body: |2-
  In zippy mode, typing a key sequence K runs what C-c K runs, and
  typing inserts no text: 'r r' cycles the processed mark, 'v d' opens the
  diff view, 'C-u r r' or '4 r r' passes a prefix argument. 'z' leaves
  zippy (skg-zippy-exit).

  The mode is global: C-c z turns it on in every skg buffer at once
  (content views, reports, buffers visiting .skg files, and those
  opened later), z turns it off in all of them, and other buffers are
  never affected. While it is on, those buffers show " Zippy" in the
  mode line, and while the selected window shows one of them the
  cursor is green (the variable 'skg-zippy-cursor-color'; the cursor
  keeps its shape and blinking, and elsewhere, and when zippy is off, its
  usual colour).

  Each buffer uses its own kind's C-c keymap itself, so a key later
  bound under C-c works in zippy too. Only skg's C-c keys count; org's
  are left out. Digits and - give a prefix argument, any other printable
  key that C-c does not bind just says so, and every other key (C-c
  itself, so C-c C-c and C-c v d still work, C-x, M-x, motion, C-g)
  works as usual. Control keys bound under C-c are zippy keys too: C-o
  follows a link (C-c C-o), and <backspace> marks a node for deletion
  (C-c <backspace>).
